<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    The iOS face of the mobile shell — the peer of Shenora.Android, sharing its source verbatim via
    the import below. Today the two differ in nothing but their TFM; the first real divergence is
    expected in the save picker (Android needs SAF, iOS needs UIDocumentPickerViewController), and it
    will land in `Platforms/` on each side rather than behind an #if.

    ⚠ This project can only be BUILT on macOS (Xcode). `dev.mjs verify` on Windows therefore never
    compiles it, and its API baseline is gated on macOS only — see MetadataSurfaceTests.
  -->
  <Import Project="..\Shenora.Mobile\Shenora.Mobile.props" />

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFrameworks>net10.0-ios</TargetFrameworks>
    <!-- 15.0 is the .NET 10 iOS workload's floor; MAUI's own template uses the same. -->
    <SupportedOSPlatformVersion>15.0</SupportedOSPlatformVersion>
    <!-- Inline on purpose — the baseline coverage gate text-matches it here. See Shenora.Mobile.props. -->
    <IsPackable>true</IsPackable>
    <PackageId>Shenora.iOS</PackageId>
    <Description>Shenora's iOS shell: the MAUI HybridWebView IPC transport (the same envelope the desktop shell speaks, over SendRawMessage/RawMessageReceived), a UI-thread dispatcher, and the iOS implementations of the Shenora.Core shell contracts. The platform owns the loop, so the app drives ShenoraApplication.Start/Stop from its own lifecycle rather than calling Run. Shares its source with Shenora.Android. Also ships the Live Activity devkit: the app writes four SwiftUI view bodies and sets one MSBuild property, and the package supplies the ActivityKit shim, the state mirror and the build step that produces and signs the widget extension.</Description>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    The Live Activity devkit's two shipped artifacts. Both go under buildTransitive/ so a PackageReference
    picks the targets up automatically and the Swift sits beside it at a path the targets can name.

    ⚠ The SWIFT IS SHIPPED AS SOURCE and that is forced, not a style choice: ActivityKit pairs a running
    activity with a widget by its ActivityAttributes TYPE, and a Swift type's identity includes its MODULE —
    so the attributes must be compiled into the same module as the app's own views. A prebuilt binary could
    never satisfy that. Measured; see .claude/knowledge/mobile-shells.md.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <None Include="buildTransitive\Shenora.iOS.targets" Pack="true" PackagePath="buildTransitive\" />
    <!--
      ⚠ The repo path and the PackagePath are deliberately IDENTICAL (`buildTransitive/swift/`). The targets
      find this by `$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)swift/`, so making the source tree mirror the package layout
      means the path the sample exercises is the path a real consumer gets — rather than two paths where only
      one is ever tested. Getting this wrong is how the first Mac build failed.
    -->
    <None Include="buildTransitive\swift\ShenoraLiveActivity.swift" Pack="true"
          PackagePath="buildTransitive\swift\" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
